  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "For the elliptic paraboloid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3}=z+4$, the section in the plane $\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4$ is a parabola. \nFind the vertex of the projection of this parabola onto $yz$-plane.",
  "worked_solution": "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}x-2y=4\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the paraboloid, we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{(\\frac{2(y+2)}{\\sqrt{3}})}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{4(y+2)^{2}}{3\\cdot 2}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{2(y+2)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ \\frac{3(y+2)^{2}}{3} & =z+4 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =z+4 \\\\ z & =(y+2)^{2}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the vertex of the projection onto $yz$-plane is $(-2,-4).$",
